  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Snag Pin (Mycocalicium subtile) is a tiny crustose lichen in the Sphinctrinaceae family, characterized by its inconspicuous thallus and distinctive pin-like fruiting bodies. Found across New England, it typically colonizes the bark of dead or dying trees, particularly in moist, shaded woodlands.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Pin-like Apothecia",
      "description": "Distinctive dark, needle-like or pin-shaped fruiting bodies that protrude from the surface, giving the species its common name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Crustose Thallus",
      "description": "Thin, tightly adhered crusty appearance that is often gray or pale brown and barely visible to the naked eye",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Substrate Preference",
      "description": "Primarily found on dead or decaying bark of hardwood trees, particularly in moist microhabitats",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Mycocalicium subtile thrives on the bark of dead, dying, or recently fallen hardwoods in cool, humid forests and woodlands throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its microscopic size and easily overlooked appearance, Snag Pin is an important pioneer lichen that helps break down dead wood and prepare substrates for other organisms in forest ecosystems.",
